{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 29, :comments ["<b>Fill.</b> It is not known what these ordinations signify, and if it concerns the killing of their relatives — behold, they had already done what they did. And the commentators said that this verse speaks of what was said before the deed, and it is difficult: why did it not write it in its place?" "And it appears that this verse is in its proper place, and the intention is because the children of Levi performed the deed stated nearby — that they killed about three thousand men — and it is a deed that is not ordinarily done through the agency of the righteous, as is known. Go out and learn from the statement of our Sages of blessed memory (Makkot 7a), who said: A court that would execute once in seven years — and some say once in seventy years — was called a murderous court. Yet the children of Levi killed on a single day about three thousand men. And there is reason to fear in this a deficiency of happiness, and a sign of diminishment: that the lights of the soul of the one who does such a thing would be diminished — even if he kills according to the law, at the end of the day this would demonstrate the cruelty which is an aspect of evil. Therefore the man of Elohim came and said to them: <b>Fill your hands</b> — its meaning is not in the language of a commandment but rather a declaration, for this day your hands — meaning, your strength — will be filled for HaShem, and in this deed there is no indication of a deficiency of happiness; quite the contrary, it is a filling of the lights of the soul. And the reason is <b>for each man against his son</b> etc., and this is the most excellent deed, comparable to our father Abraham, in which he attained perfection, as it is written (Genesis 22:10): ״וַיִּשְׁלַח אַבְרָהָם אֶת יָדוֹ״ etc., and it is written: ״עַתָּה יָדַעְתִּי״ etc. ״כִּי בָרֵךְ אֲבָרֶכְךָ״. And this is what he says: <b>and to bestow upon you this day a blessing</b> — besides that through this you attained the perfection of the hand of holiness, furthermore <b>to bestow upon you a blessing.</b>" "<b>Further,</b> it intends to address the fine point of the word <b>upon you</b> — and he did not say 'to you' — to convey as well that the diminishment that occurred through their hands is not called a diminishment, but quite the contrary: they brought about blessing through their deeds. And this is like a tree that one prunes for its success by removing the bad branches; likewise, through the killing of evildoers from among the children of Israel, who are likened to a vine (Psalms 80:9). And this is what he says: <b>and to bestow upon you</b> — meaning, on account of you — <b>this day a blessing</b> — meaning, to Israel, in whom the diminishment occurred — for without this, the entire house of Israel would have been in danger. And accordingly, the reason the Sages said [that a court executing] once in seventy years etc. is in a situation that is not comparable to the situation before us, for two reasons: first, because here they killed their beloved ones, and this demonstrates the extraordinary degree of their love for the Creator; and furthermore, the situation was such that but for this, evil would have cleaved to all Israel, and through this HaShem turned back from His wrath."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 32, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:36:24.553090332Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}